WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.353
Here is a delivery robot at

2
00:00:05.444 --> 00:00:07.352
the corner of a grid.

3
00:00:07.352 --> 00:00:09.168
It knows two moves.

4
00:00:09.259 --> 00:00:11.531
Move a goes one step east

5
00:00:11.621 --> 00:00:13.620
and three steps north.

6
00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:16.431
Move b goes two steps east

7
00:00:16.526 --> 00:00:18.324
and one step south.

8
00:00:18.324 --> 00:00:22.300
We want it to stop at the charging point

9
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:23.530
seven, zero.

10
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:27.334
How many a moves, and how many b moves?

11
00:00:27.334 --> 00:00:28.840
Pause and guess.

12
00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:35.100
Call the number of a moves x

13
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.780
and the number of b moves y.

14
00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.756
Count the steps east first.

15
00:00:40.756 --> 00:00:44.257
Each a gives one, each b gives two

16
00:00:44.257 --> 00:00:46.104
and we need seven

17
00:00:46.104 --> 00:00:48.730
x plus two y equals seven.

18
00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.752
Now count north.

19
00:00:50.752 --> 00:00:55.247
Each a gives three, each b takes one away

20
00:00:55.247 --> 00:00:57.129
and we need zero

21
00:00:57.129 --> 00:01:00.160
three x minus y equals zero.

22
00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:03.120
Two unknowns and two equations

23
00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.150
one for each direction on the grid.

24
00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:08.824
The first way to read this

25
00:01:08.905 --> 00:01:10.435
is the row picture.

26
00:01:10.435 --> 00:01:13.978
It lives on a different plane: across is x

27
00:01:13.978 --> 00:01:16.878
the number of a moves, and up is y

28
00:01:16.878 --> 00:01:18.730
the number of b moves.

29
00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:22.030
Every point here is one possible answer.

30
00:01:22.103 --> 00:01:24.389
The pairs that satisfy the east

31
00:01:24.463 --> 00:01:26.675
equation fill a straight line.

32
00:01:26.675 --> 00:01:29.920
Seven, zero is on it, and so is three, two.

33
00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:32.896
The north equation is a second line.

34
00:01:32.969 --> 00:01:35.668
It goes through the origin and climbs

35
00:01:35.741 --> 00:01:37.710
three for every one across.

36
00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.085
An answer to both equations

37
00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:41.885
must sit on both lines

38
00:01:41.885 --> 00:01:44.060
so we look where they cross.

39
00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.140
They cross at one, three.

40
00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.340
Check east

41
00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:51.840
one plus two times three is seven.

42
00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.140
Check north

43
00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:56.840
three times one minus three is zero.

44
00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:58.431
Both hold.

45
00:01:58.431 --> 00:02:03.393
So x is one and y is three: one a move

46
00:02:03.393 --> 00:02:05.750
and three b moves.

47
00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:09.130
But look at this picture once more.

48
00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:12.570
The robot's two moves, the arrows a and b

49
00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:14.090
are nowhere in it.

50
00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:17.467
The lines came from the equations.

51
00:02:17.467 --> 00:02:19.960
So where did the arrows go?

52
00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:25.428
They come back in the column picture.

53
00:02:25.428 --> 00:02:27.614
Return to the robot's grid

54
00:02:27.692 --> 00:02:30.112
and read the equations down the

55
00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:32.220
columns instead of across.

56
00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:36.653
The numbers beside x are one and three

57
00:02:36.653 --> 00:02:38.328
that is move a.

58
00:02:38.328 --> 00:02:42.935
The numbers beside y are two and minus one

59
00:02:42.935 --> 00:02:44.610
that is move b.

60
00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:48.539
So the system asks a single question

61
00:02:48.636 --> 00:02:51.248
x copies of a plus y copies

62
00:02:51.345 --> 00:02:54.440
of b should land on seven, zero.

63
00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:58.541
Test the amounts from the row picture

64
00:02:58.541 --> 00:03:02.340
one a and three b's, placed tip to tail.

65
00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:05.608
One a move reaches one, three.

66
00:03:05.608 --> 00:03:07.456
One b: three, two.

67
00:03:07.456 --> 00:03:09.401
Another: five, one.

68
00:03:09.401 --> 00:03:11.930
And a third: seven, zero.

69
00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:13.920
Right on the target.

70
00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.791
One a plus three b is seven, zero

71
00:03:16.791 --> 00:03:20.400
and the order of the moves does not matter.

72
00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:23.652
Why is there only one answer?

73
00:03:23.744 --> 00:03:26.416
Every amount of a lies on one

74
00:03:26.508 --> 00:03:28.627
line through the start

75
00:03:28.627 --> 00:03:32.220
and every amount of b lies on another.

76
00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:35.839
The two lines point in different directions

77
00:03:35.913 --> 00:03:38.587
so each point of the grid is reached

78
00:03:38.662 --> 00:03:40.890
by exactly one mix of a and b.

79
00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:42.912
Here is a trap.

80
00:03:42.912 --> 00:03:45.411
Our answer is one, three

81
00:03:45.411 --> 00:03:48.390
and move a is also one, three.

82
00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:52.567
That match is a coincidence of this example.

83
00:03:52.567 --> 00:03:56.133
The answer is not an arrow on this grid.

84
00:03:56.133 --> 00:03:57.524
It is a recipe

85
00:03:57.524 --> 00:04:00.220
how much of each arrow to use.

86
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:03.764
Move the target to four, five.

87
00:04:03.764 --> 00:04:08.111
Make one b move, then one a, and another a.

88
00:04:08.111 --> 00:04:10.680
That lands on four, five.

89
00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:13.311
So the recipe is x equals

90
00:04:13.402 --> 00:04:15.318
two and y equals one

91
00:04:15.318 --> 00:04:18.329
which looks nothing like a or b.

92
00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:22.070
Amounts and arrows are different things.

93
00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.455
So one system gives two pictures.

94
00:04:25.455 --> 00:04:29.195
In the row picture each equation is a line

95
00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:31.575
and the answer is the point

96
00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:33.530
where the lines cross.

97
00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:36.588
In the column picture each unknown

98
00:04:36.668 --> 00:04:38.818
is an amount of one column

99
00:04:38.898 --> 00:04:41.288
and the answer is the mix that

100
00:04:41.367 --> 00:04:42.960
lands on the target.

101
00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:45.948
Both pictures give the same answer

102
00:04:45.948 --> 00:04:48.510
x equals one, and y equals three.

103
00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:50.986
With two unknowns the row

104
00:04:51.062 --> 00:04:52.882
picture is easy to draw.

105
00:04:52.882 --> 00:04:55.992
With many unknowns, lines become planes

106
00:04:55.992 --> 00:04:58.570
and the picture gets hard to see.

107
00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:01.723
The column picture keeps working

108
00:05:01.811 --> 00:05:03.299
in any dimension.

109
00:05:03.299 --> 00:05:03.825
Next

110
00:05:03.825 --> 00:05:07.328
we pack both pictures into one product

111
00:05:07.328 --> 00:05:09.080
A times x equals b.
